From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arsslen Idadi <arsslens021@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:12:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/18] focaltech_moh: add a matcher that decides on minutiae

The matcher this driver has scores a probe by normalised cross-correlation
with each enrolled press over a translation search. On these sensors that
cannot be made safe. A press shows a few millimetres of finger, where ridges
mostly run parallel, and parallel ridges carry little identity: two
different fingers line up almost perfectly once their angle, spacing and
phase are matched, which is all a correlation search tunes. Every bound on
the scores proposed for it has been broken by an adjacent finger of the
same hand. Measured on synthetic fingerprints of a 96x96 press sensor (the
method of SFinGe: zero-pole orientation fields, ridges grown by Gabor
filtering, presses with placement, rotation, skin distortion, dry patches
and noise), it accepts a wrong finger about once in 550 presses, and rejects
43% of the right finger's.

What tells fingers apart on this little skin is where ridges end and split.
focaltech_moh_match.c therefore:

- normalises a press's contrast and masks it to where ridges show;
- finds its minutiae: ridge orientation from smoothed gradients, contextual
  Gabor enhancement at the press's ridge period, Zhang-Suen thinning,
  crossing numbers, and pairs of minutiae closer than 7 px dropped as
  skeleton artefacts;
- aligns a probe with an enrolled press by masked NCC (Padfield, "Masked
  object registration in the Fourier domain", 2012) over rotations of up to
  24 degrees, coarse to fine, and shifts of up to 24 px whose overlap covers
  54% of the frame;
- and only then pairs their minutiae at that pose, within 4 px and 20
  degrees. The pose is chosen without looking at minutiae, so a wrong
  finger pairs them only by chance.

A press aligned at NCC >= 0.5 gives as evidence its paired minutiae less the
unpaired ones, on both sides, where the other press could show them. A
genuine probe agrees with several enrolled presses, which cover the same
skin; a wrong finger's chance pairings come from one coincidental alignment
and are not repeated by another press. So the two best presses' evidence
together must reach 5. A probe covering less than 80% of the sensor, or
with fewer than 4 minutiae, is not decided on at all.

The rule was chosen on one synthetic set and measured on another (2200
fingers, ten per person, 15 enrolled presses each): of 246753 usable presses
of another finger, 148086 of them of the same person, none was accepted, a
95% upper bound of 1 in 82000; one reached 4. A usable press of the right
finger is accepted 73% of the time, and 18% of presses are not usable.

A print is its enrolled presses, each the raw frame and its minutiae, so
that the matcher can be improved without enrolling again; reading one
trusts nothing in it. Matching a press against 15 takes some 0.1 s.

test-focaltech-moh-match runs it on synthetic presses (a finger, another
person's finger, and a twin with the same ridge flow): genuine probes are
accepted, impostors and perfect parallel ridges are not, flat and noise
frames are safe, enrolled presses link, and prints round-trip, refuse every
malformed field and survive random corruption.
